# Goal: identical changes on both branches still produce a merge commit.
I setup2.inc
> branch other
<<<
+ f.txt notwug.txt
> add f.txt
<<<
> commit "Change f on master"
<<<
> checkout other
<<<
+ f.txt notwug.txt
> add f.txt
<<<
> commit "Same change on other"
<<<
> checkout master
<<<
> merge other
<<<
= f.txt notwug.txt
= g.txt notwug.txt
> log
===
${COMMIT_HEAD}
Merged other into master\.

${ARBLINES}
<<<*
I blank-status2.inc
> merge other
Given branch is an ancestor of the current branch.
<<<

# Rule 3: if both branches deleted a tracked path, a recreated work file is
# preserved as untracked during the normal merge.
C BOTH-DELETED
> init
<<<
+ x.txt wug.txt
> add x.txt
<<<
> commit "track x"
<<<
> branch given
<<<
> rm x.txt
<<<
+ master.txt notwug.txt
> add master.txt
<<<
> commit "master deletes x"
<<<
> checkout given
<<<
> rm x.txt
<<<
+ given.txt wug2.txt
> add given.txt
<<<
> commit "given deletes x"
<<<
> checkout master
<<<
+ x.txt wug3.txt
> merge given
<<<
= x.txt wug3.txt
> status
=== Branches ===
given
*master

=== Staged Files ===

=== Removed Files ===

=== Modifications Not Staged For Commit ===

=== Untracked Files ===
x.txt

<<<

# An ancestor merge performs no worktree check or write, even when an
# untracked file has the same path as one tracked by that ancestor.
C ANCESTOR
> init
<<<
+ x.txt wug.txt
> add x.txt
<<<
> commit "track x"
<<<
> branch old
<<<
> rm x.txt
<<<
> commit "remove x"
<<<
+ x.txt wug2.txt
> merge old
Given branch is an ancestor of the current branch.
<<<
= x.txt wug2.txt
> status
=== Branches ===
*master
old

=== Staged Files ===

=== Removed Files ===

=== Modifications Not Staged For Commit ===

=== Untracked Files ===
x.txt

<<<
